{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The problem explicitly asks the arguer to identify what evidence would distinguish failure modes that are truly independent from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. SRC_001 never addresses this evidentiary question at all. It redefines resilience in terms of design diversity and argues that component redundancy is illusory against shared design flaws, but it provides no discussion of what observations, tests, or evidence would let one tell apart a set of node failures that are genuinely independent from a set that only looks independent because the shared cause has not yet been discovered. This is a direct omission of a required component of the problem as stated.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues against decentralization by asserting that a large service can implement 'bridge-population fail-safes' isolating redundant components behind strict boundaries, but it never engages with the problem's core requirement: identifying evidence that distinguishes truly independent failures from merely-uncorrelated-until-shared-cause failures. Its own countercondition even concedes the fail-safe itself may be a shared component that can fail (a correlated point), yet the main argument never explains how one would empirically determine whether the decentralized network's node failures are truly independent or only appear so until the shared foundational zero-day is discovered. The argument thus fails half the stated problem while also leaving its key large-service claim internally undercut by its own listed countercondition.","counterexample":null}]}